# cssom-heads-styleguide.baseline — the exact authored-rule-head count of the
# INLINE <style> sheet inside cloud/priv/static/styleguide.html.
#
# Sibling of cssom-heads.baseline, same ratchet, different file. cssom-parity.mjs
# asserts authoredHeads(styleguide.html's inline sheet) == this number (EXACT, not
# >=), for the reasons its own "THE EXACT-MATCH SIDECAR RATCHET" block gives: a
# `/*` swallow drops the count and reds, a new rule raises it and reds, until the
# number is bumped IN THE SAME COMMIT as the CSS change.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Through wave 7 cssom-parity certified app.css and
# nothing else, while styleguide.html shipped 193 lines of CSS in an inline
# <style> block (lines 10-202 of 841) and sat in the Plug.Static `only:` allowlist
# in BarkparkCloud.Web.Router — genuinely served, and parsed by no gate. The one
# instrument that opens that block, __css_check.mjs, is pointed the other way: its
# E2 pass harvests the page's class="…" attributes and asks whether APP.CSS
# defines them, with the inline sheet's own selectors EXEMPTED by design
# (sgLocalClasses); E12 reads it for `:focus` rules only and prints, honestly, that
# the population is empty. So the sheet's own 84 rules had never been parsed by
# anything. Bug #4592 could have happened there in exactly the same way.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE SIDECAR RATHER THAN ONE SHARED NUMBER. A single count over two
# files can only ever be a sum, and a sum is a number no reviewer can bump
# honestly: it moves for a reason in a file the diff may not even touch. Per-sheet
# counts keep each bump attributable to the file it is about.
#
# Measured 2026-09-01 on origin/main @ 1caa2316a7, by a real cssom-parity.mjs run
# (Chrome 152.0.7977.65, node v22.22.0), not hand-derived:
#   styleguide.html · 52722 B · sha256 ff7b951c041e…
#   authored rule heads 84 == 84 CSSOM style rules, 82/82 flattened, MISSES 0.
#
# THE COUNT IS OF RULE HEADS IN THE INLINE SHEET ONLY. The `<link rel="stylesheet"
# href="app.css">` this page also carries is app.css's business and is counted by
# the sibling sidecar; feeding both into one page would sum the two censuses and
# make each number a statement about neither file.
84
